        <div id="which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Car Battery Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>For a start-stop car or anything that came with AGM,</strong> buy a mainstream AGM in the exact group size: the DieHard Platinum AGM if you have an Advance Auto Parts nearby, the Duracell Ultra Platinum AGM if you already shop at a warehouse club, or the Interstate MTX if you prefer a dealer or installer network. All three are aimed at the same job. Choose on freshness, warranty terms and convenience of exchange, and remember to have the battery registered if your car requires it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For trucks, cold climates and vehicles that sit,</strong> the Odyssey Extreme Series is the premium pick. Its pure-lead AGM design is built for strong cranking and slow self-discharge, which suits a pickup that tows in winter, a weekend car that sits for weeks, or a work vehicle with a long list of accessories. It is expensive and heavy, and in a commuter car it is more battery than you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For older performance cars, off-road rigs and vehicles with vibration problems,</strong> the Optima RedTop is the long-standing choice. Its spiral-cell construction is famously tough and it mounts in almost any orientation. If you run a winch, a big audio system or other accessories that pull the battery down regularly, the Optima YellowTop is the better Optima, because it is designed for repeated deep discharge as well as starting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For an older car without start-stop on a budget,</strong> the EverStart Maxx is the sensible buy. It is a conventional flooded battery with a reasonable free-replacement period and an exchange counter in nearly every town. Check the date code carefully, because shelf age matters more with flooded batteries, and do not put it in a car that came with AGM or EFB.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>If you are reading Duracell auto battery reviews and wondering whether the name means anything,</strong> know that Duracell licenses its brand for automotive batteries made by an established battery manufacturer; you are buying the factory and the warranty, not the company that makes AA cells. That is true of most store and licensed brands, and it is fine. What separates the best rated auto battery from the rest in practice is the right group, the right technology, a recent date code and a warranty you can actually use. Pair it with a jump starter or a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">power station</a> and a smart maintainer, and a dead battery stops being an emergency.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by CCA instead of group size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cold-cranking amps only matter once the battery fits. The group size fixes the dimensions and terminal layout, and the wrong group can mean a loose hold-down, stretched cables or reversed polarity. Match the group in your owner's manual or on the old battery first, then pick a battery that meets or slightly exceeds the minimum CCA.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Downgrading a start-stop car to flooded</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If your car came with an AGM or EFB battery, a conventional flooded replacement will usually fail early and can make start-stop stop working or trigger warnings. Replace like with like or better, and check whether the car needs the new battery registered with its computer so the charging system adjusts correctly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the date code</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lead-acid batteries self-discharge on the shelf, and one that sat for a year can arrive weakened. Check the sticker or stamped code before paying and aim for a battery built within the last six months, preferably three. If the store's stock is old, ask for a fresh one or have it tested and charged first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing the battery without testing the charging system</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A battery that keeps dying is often the victim, not the cause. A failing alternator, a parasitic drain or corroded cables will kill a new battery just as quickly. Have the battery, starter and alternator tested, which most parts stores do free, before assuming the battery is the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to jump-start with a power station</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A portable power station's 12V output is typically limited to around 10 amps, far below what a starter draws, so it will trip rather than crank. Use a dedicated jump starter or cables, or use the power station's AC outlet to run a small battery charger and bring the battery back up slowly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

